# Everything listed here is excluded from the build context — the set of files
# Docker uploads to the daemon before building.
#
# This file matters more than it looks. A bloated context slows every build and,
# worse, invalidates layer caches: if .venv is in the context, touching any
# installed package busts the cache and forces a full reinstall. It is also the
# main defence against baking secrets into an image.

# The virtual environment is host-specific and huge. The image builds its own
# from uv.lock, which is the whole point of committing a lockfile.
.venv/

# Git history — hundreds of megabytes in a mature repo, useless at runtime, and
# a common way that deleted-but-still-in-history secrets end up in an image.
.git/
.gitignore

# Python bytecode, rebuilt inside the image.
__pycache__/
**/__pycache__/
*.py[oc]
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/

# Training data. The serving image needs neither the raw download nor the
# training and validation splits — SHAP's background distribution is saved next
# to the model at training time precisely so serving does not depend on them.
# test.csv is kept deliberately: the dashboard's worklist tab scores it as demo
# data. A production image would drop it and read customers from the warehouse.
data/raw/
data/processed/
data/interim/train.csv
data/interim/val.csv

# Experiment tracking. Runs and their artifacts belong on a tracking server, not
# inside a serving image.
mlruns/
mlartifacts/
mlflow.db

# Generated figures and search histories. final_evaluation.json is NOT excluded:
# the API reads the operating threshold from it.
reports/figures/
reports/cv_results_*.csv
reports/explainability.json

# Logs are written at runtime.
logs/

# Local tooling and editor state.
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store
.ipynb_checkpoints/
notebooks/
tests/
docs/

# Secrets. Belt and braces — these should never exist in the repo, but an
# image is forever and a leaked key is expensive.
.env
*.pem
*.key
